From the original author of uIP:
"This directory contains a few example applications. They are not all
heavily tested, however."

Of the original UIP sample apps:

dhcpd 		- builds and runs on the Dragon
hello-world - builds and runs on the Dragon
resolv		- builds and runs on the Dragon
smtp		- builds and runs on the Dragon
telnetd		- builds and runs on the Dragon
webclient	- builds and runs on the Dragon
webserver	- builds and runs on the Dragon. Enabling this app will 
result in quite a large binary, you'll need to adjust the program
origin address down to something like 0x1500 in the Makefile and 
most likely won't be able to include any of the others at the same time

New apps added by me:

solar-udp	- builds and runs on the Dragon
weather-udp	- builds and runs on the Dragon
callchain	- builds and runs on the Dragon
ntpclient   - builds and runs on the Dragon

To enable an application, add it to the APPS list in the top level Makefile.

As per the original UIP documentation, normally there is only support for 
inclusion of a single application per protocol (TCP or UDP). The application
'callchain' serves as a means of circumventing this limitation by simply
daisy-chaining calls to the applications that have been enabled. This is a 
little bit messy to manage, pay particular attention to the comments and
structure of includes in uip-conf.h

UDP support is only enabled if an application is included that requires it, see
the conditional in uip-conf.h. By default, this also unconditionally enables
UDP broadcast support. 

Both the Dragon and Linux (unix) builds should operate more or less in the same
fashion now although some of the combinations of things hasn't be rolled into 
the later.

Be aware when building on the Dragon, if you enable multiple applications, you 
may need to adjust the program origin address to avoid running out of memory. 
You *may* also run into performance problems as well (receiver buffer overruns)
if you ask it to do too much!
